WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:05.160
Let's talk about the Unreal Build Tool or the UBT.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.040
As we've just seen,

3
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.279
Unreal is fundamentally split into modules

4
00:00:08.279 --> 00:00:10.820
and the engine has got a lot of them.

5
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.259
This tool essentially regenerates your project solution

6
00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:16.179
by iterating over all build.cs files,

7
00:00:16.179 --> 00:00:19.100
discovering new DLLs that it needs to build,

8
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.579
as well as adding any new target.cs files

9
00:00:21.579 --> 00:00:24.120
that you may have added as configuration options.

10
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.500
The Unreal Build Tool runs totally independently

11
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.980
from any development environment

12
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.739
and can be accessed through a contextual button

13
00:00:29.739 --> 00:00:31.260
on the U project file.

14
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.500
You should rerun the UBT every time files are added

15
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.619
or removed from the project.

16
00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:39.479
Let's take a look at what it does under the hood.

17
00:00:39.479 --> 00:00:42.840
When we initialize the build tool, the following happens.

18
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.560
The UBT takes a project, target platform,

19
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:46.840
and a configuration,

20
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.399
and then gathers all of the target.cs files

21
00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:52.719
and the build.cs files for its modules.

22
00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.000
With the specified target,

23
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.840
it will create an instance of that target

24
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.079
and then find all of the modules, source code,

25
00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:01.619
and plugins required to successfully build that target.

26
00:01:01.619 --> 00:01:04.299
We'll then look for all the header files for the modules,

27
00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:05.819
looking specifically for files

28
00:01:05.819 --> 00:01:08.750
that contain Unreal-related metadata.

29
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.010
It then passes those header files

30
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.370
to the Unreal header tool or the UHT for parsing

31
00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:15.010
to create the generated code

32
00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.510
which gets fed back into the Unreal Build Tool.

33
00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.689
The UBT then generates an action graph,

34
00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:22.629
which is a graph full of all the compilation

35
00:01:22.629 --> 00:01:24.109
and linking operations needed

36
00:01:24.109 --> 00:01:26.189
to be performed to build the desired project

37
00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:28.930
for a specified target and configuration.

38
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.969
The action graph, which are essentially just cl.exe

39
00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:35.010
or link.exe nodes with parameters applied,

40
00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.329
gets saved into a makefile

41
00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:37.530
so that it doesn't need to keep doing

42
00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.590
these steps constantly.

43
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.549
It will then execute the action graph to build two projects

44
00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:45.829
with an executer to compile all .obj files

45
00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:50.230
and link them to .exe or .dlls and PDBs.

46
00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:51.409
Realistically, as engineers,

47
00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:53.670
we're unlikely to interact with either the UBT

48
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.030
or the UHT directly,

49
00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:57.670
aside from rerunning it when we update our project.

50
00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:58.790
But it's important that we at least

51
00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:01.430
have a cursory knowledge of what happens under the hood

52
00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.370
before going forward.

53
00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:06.689
Unlike raw C++, Unreal's flavor of C++

54
00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:08.389
fully supports reflection,

55
00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:12.120
and for the most part, it revolves around the UObject.

56
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.560
The Unreal Reflection System,

57
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.039
which is otherwise known as the Property System,

58
00:02:16.039 --> 00:02:18.639
is an opt-in system that gives the program

59
00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:21.360
the ability to examine itself at runtime.

60
00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.659
This system not only powers our game projects,

61
00:02:23.659 --> 00:02:27.069
but also the engine and its constituent parts.

62
00:02:27.069 --> 00:02:29.550
All reflected types are based off of UObject,

63
00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.250
with UObject being the lowest class

64
00:02:31.289 --> 00:02:34.590
that we can get to in terms of the Unreal file structure.

65
00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.150
Every player, game object, bullet, visual effects, sound cue,

66
00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:42.520
their class hierarchy all eventually lead to UObject.

67
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.560
We can't mention UObject

68
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.240
without pointing out that U prefix.

69
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.840
Unreal has got a strongly enforced naming convention system

70
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.139
that lets us know what the class we're working with

71
00:02:51.139 --> 00:02:52.840
is currently based on.

72
00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.719
So as an example, we can see the child classes

73
00:02:54.719 --> 00:02:56.479
that are not based off of actor

74
00:02:56.479 --> 00:02:59.960
have got the U prefix, such as the UData asset.

75
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.979
Actor-derived types have the A prefix,

76
00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:04.270
such as the A exploding barrel.

77
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:07.469
Structs or non-UObject classes have got the F prefix,

78
00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:11.159
such as the F explosion params or the F archive.

79
00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:13.000
Enums have got the E prefix,

80
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.039
and templated types have got the T prefix,

81
00:03:15.039 --> 00:03:16.819
such as T my templated class,

82
00:03:16.819 --> 00:03:19.949
or T unique pointer with a template being passed in.

83
00:03:19.949 --> 00:03:21.289
To create and mark a file

84
00:03:21.289 --> 00:03:23.349
as containing a reflected system type,

85
00:03:23.349 --> 00:03:25.469
the following must be added to the class.

86
00:03:25.469 --> 00:03:26.729
We add a special include,

87
00:03:26.729 --> 00:03:31.400
that being the hash include of myFileName.generated.h,

88
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.979
we then mark the class with the U class markup,

89
00:03:33.979 --> 00:03:37.139
a struct with a U struct, or an enum with U enum.

90
00:03:37.139 --> 00:03:38.080
We then add the following

91
00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.039
within the class declaration body.

92
00:03:40.039 --> 00:03:42.659
We add the generated body macro.

93
00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:45.020
This will generate typedefs and additional functions,

94
00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:47.340
such as static class and static struct,

95
00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.780
that are used in the reflection system

96
00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.939
to retrieve reflection data from this class.

97
00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:52.780
And that's it.

98
00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.509
This type is now part of the Unreal reflection system.

99
00:03:55.509 --> 00:03:58.250
And the editor can actually do this for you.

100
00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.389
The editor contains a new class wizard.

101
00:04:00.389 --> 00:04:02.750
It will generate both a header and a CPP file

102
00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.310
in a specified location,

103
00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.699
and add all of this boilerplate for you.

104
00:04:06.699 --> 00:04:08.939
The above will mark this class as a reflected type,

105
00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:10.939
and it will be subject to that parsing phase

106
00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:13.719
of the Unreal build tool that we spoke about previously.

107
00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:15.400
That being the phase that was looking for files

108
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.480
containing Unreal metadata.

109
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:19.480
As well as U class,

110
00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.959
we can also mark up many other parts of our code

111
00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:23.519
to pass along to the UBT,

112
00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:26.920
giving us extended functionality such as editor visibility

113
00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.279
or smart memory management.

114
00:04:29.319 --> 00:04:31.560
We can mark up member variables with U property

115
00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.459
to prevent garbage collection from nulling out the pointer,

116
00:04:34.459 --> 00:04:35.779
or functions with U function

117
00:04:35.779 --> 00:04:38.680
to make them callable from blueprint.

118
00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.519
U class allows engineers to mark up members within

119
00:04:41.519 --> 00:04:42.920
with additional macros,

120
00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.639
as well as provide additional class specifies to this class,

121
00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:47.120
expanding its use.

122
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.000
For example, we have blueprint type,

123
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.319
blueprintable, and abstract,

124
00:04:51.319 --> 00:04:52.759
but there are many other options available

125
00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:56.839
and a full list can be found inside objectmacros.h.

126
00:04:56.839 --> 00:04:59.399
The U property markup is applied to member variables

127
00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:00.639
within a U class,

128
00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:03.879
and much like U class lets us specify property specifies

129
00:05:03.879 --> 00:05:05.639
for a particular member.

130
00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:07.680
Critically, it also prevents garbage collection

131
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.079
from nulling out any cache pointers.

132
00:05:10.079 --> 00:05:11.560
We don't have to manage our own memory

133
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.939
when working with U properties.

134
00:05:12.939 --> 00:05:14.759
They're being reference counted for us.

135
00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:17.040
As long as they're flagged up with the U property macro,

136
00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.259
we should be fine.

137
00:05:18.259 --> 00:05:20.680
Some examples of specifies that we would typically see

138
00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.360
inside a U project would be edit anywhere.

139
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.009
This value is exposed to property windows in the editor.

140
00:05:27.009 --> 00:05:28.129
Edit defaults only.

141
00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.250
This value is exposed to property windows,

142
00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.410
but only in archetypes.

143
00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.410
Visible anywhere.

144
00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.029
This value is visible, but cannot be edited.

145
00:05:36.029 --> 00:05:37.029
Replicated.

146
00:05:37.029 --> 00:05:39.610
This value is liable to network replication.

147
00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:40.889
Additional setup is required

148
00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:42.389
to actually get it to replicate,

149
00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:44.819
but this is just the first step towards getting there.

150
00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:46.100
And then finally we've got transient,

151
00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.300
which basically means this property is not serialized

152
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.560
and will be zero filled at runtime.

153
00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:52.660
As with U class,

154
00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:56.939
objectmacros.h has got a more definitive list.

155
00:05:56.939 --> 00:05:58.680
Having discussed the U property macro

156
00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.420
and how we can use it to prevent our pointers

157
00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:01.959
from being garbage collected,

158
00:06:01.959 --> 00:06:04.040
it's also important that we discuss a new addition

159
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.699
to Unreal Engine 5, the T object pointer.

160
00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:08.980
Firstly, it's important to note

161
00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:10.180
that the use of raw pointers

162
00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:13.100
is still a totally valid way to store your data.

163
00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.360
However, we'd recommend the use

164
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.269
of a T object pointer instead.

165
00:06:17.269 --> 00:06:19.290
A T object pointer is the size equivalent

166
00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.149
of a 64 bit pointer,

167
00:06:21.149 --> 00:06:22.589
and will convert to a raw pointer

168
00:06:22.589 --> 00:06:26.389
when passed into functions and stored in local variables.

169
00:06:26.389 --> 00:06:28.310
Its main advantage comes in editor builds

170
00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:30.230
where it supports lazy load behavior,

171
00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.879
which will speed up your engine boot times.

172
00:06:32.879 --> 00:06:34.519
The T object pointer can also cache

173
00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:36.000
resolved object addresses.

174
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.740
So in range based iteration,

175
00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:39.879
it's recommended to use something like an auto ref

176
00:06:39.879 --> 00:06:44.839
or a const auto ref to speed up future access attempts.

177
00:06:44.839 --> 00:06:46.279
In terms of garbage collection,

178
00:06:46.279 --> 00:06:48.759
this will still work the same as a raw pointer,

179
00:06:48.759 --> 00:06:50.600
namely because we still use in conjunction

180
00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.199
with the U property macro.

181
00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:54.879
Its other advantage is that it supports

182
00:06:54.879 --> 00:06:58.050
cook time dependency tracking.

183
00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.769
This means that their use is tracked

184
00:06:59.769 --> 00:07:03.439
to build up asset to asset cook dependencies.

185
00:07:03.439 --> 00:07:05.480
The engine itself has already been converted

186
00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:06.800
to use a T object pointer.

187
00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.199
So for example, in actor,

188
00:07:08.199 --> 00:07:10.319
the root component variable is now converted

189
00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:12.399
to use a T object pointer.

190
00:07:12.399 --> 00:07:14.240
So any direct interactions with this variable

191
00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.879
will need to be updated.

192
00:07:15.879 --> 00:07:18.199
However, the get root component still returns

193
00:07:18.199 --> 00:07:21.980
the same U scene component pointer.

194
00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.300
Similar in implementation to U property,

195
00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.740
U function allows engineers

196
00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.100
to give different functionality to a function

197
00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.699
based off of the meta specified.

198
00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:31.899
For instance, blueprint callable,

199
00:07:31.899 --> 00:07:34.079
this function can be called from blueprint.

200
00:07:34.079 --> 00:07:35.379
Blueprint cosmetic,

201
00:07:35.379 --> 00:07:37.879
this function will not be run on dedicated servers.

202
00:07:37.879 --> 00:07:40.879
So think playing VFX or a sound cue.

203
00:07:40.879 --> 00:07:42.120
We've then got server,

204
00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:45.160
this function is replicated and executed on the server.

205
00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:46.600
When this is called on a client,

206
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.300
its implementation will be executed on the server.

207
00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.199
So think passing input information

208
00:07:51.199 --> 00:07:53.779
from the client back up to the server.

209
00:07:53.779 --> 00:07:55.980
We've then got client, similar to server,

210
00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.139
this function is replicated and executed on the client.

211
00:07:59.139 --> 00:08:00.500
When this is called on the server,

212
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:02.220
its implementation will be called on the client.

213
00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:06.949
So think UI updating based off of authoritative information.

214
00:08:06.949 --> 00:08:08.170
We've then got net multicast

215
00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:10.069
and this function is replicated as well.

216
00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:11.569
And when called on the server,

217
00:08:11.569 --> 00:08:13.810
the implementation is fired everywhere.

218
00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:15.889
So that would be locally on the server

219
00:08:15.889 --> 00:08:18.759
as well as on any relevant clients.

220
00:08:18.759 --> 00:08:19.759
These networked functions

221
00:08:19.759 --> 00:08:23.160
are called remote procedure calls or RPCs.

222
00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:26.560
Structs and enums also have their own reflection markup

223
00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.149
that being the U struct and U enum respectively.

224
00:08:30.709 --> 00:08:32.509
U struct, much like all other types of markup

225
00:08:32.509 --> 00:08:34.429
supports meta, the most common of which

226
00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:35.929
being the blueprint type,

227
00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:38.389
which lets us use this type in the editor.

228
00:08:38.389 --> 00:08:40.629
What it also does is allow us to mark up members

229
00:08:40.629 --> 00:08:42.629
of the struct itself as U property,

230
00:08:42.629 --> 00:08:44.590
much like we would in a U class.

231
00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.450
In this way, we can have pointers inside of structs

232
00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:50.940
with no fear of them being GC'd erroneously.

233
00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.759
U enums also support blueprint type as a meta

234
00:08:53.759 --> 00:08:55.419
and it lets us add U meta

235
00:08:55.419 --> 00:08:57.740
to individual members of the enum.

236
00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.139
For example, we can hide the max member

237
00:09:00.659 --> 00:09:02.460
of an enum from being shown in the editor

238
00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:05.740
as max members are typically only used for iteration,

239
00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:09.230
not actual gameplay designs or implementations.

240
00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:11.870
By no means are these the only reflection meta specifiers,

241
00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:13.870
they're just the most common examples.
